      "writtenOn": "2026-11-25T12:42:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The INDIRECT_STATUS table says for the ACK bit, in v1.0 and v1.1: \"If the timeout expires, then the RA must read RECOVERY_STATUS\". After `waitWriteAck()` times out, `writeImage` writes the same chunk again. If the device stored the first write and set ACK after the last INDIRECT_STATUS poll, the IMO has advanced, so the resend stores another copy of the chunk. Could `writeImage` read RECOVERY_STATUS on timeout and return an error?",
      "revId": "bddb86abc588cbab9649821e36d2749c86d508c4",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ccf6ddc3_f356694e",
        "filename": "libocp/src/ocp_recovery.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 336,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003265
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-11-25T12:42:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "v1.0 and v1.1 require PROT_CAP, and v1.1 section 8.2.5 says: \"The RA shall only access INDIRECT_CTRL/INDIRECT_STATUS/INDIRECT_DATA if PROT_CAP BIT 5 is set.\" When `getProtCap()` returns an error, or bit 5 and FIFO bit 12 are clear, `recover()` can force recovery mode and writes INDIRECT_CTRL, so a device without the register is in recovery mode and gets no image. Could `recover()` return an error in both cases, with a `Target` flag to opt out?",
      "revId": "bddb86abc588cbab9649821e36d2749c86d508c4",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    }
